<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Conolly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Conolly was a British intelligence officer and traveller in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s army who is credited with coining the phrase 'the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>' for the Anglo-Russian rivalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 1841 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> to try to free his colleague <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a>, but was himself imprisoned, and both men were beheaded on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Nasrullah in June 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly.jpg" alt="Arthur Conolly" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Atkinson (1780-1852) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArthurConolly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Conolly was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 2 July 1807, a descendant of an Ó Conghalaigh clan of Ireland and a cousin of Sir William Macnaghten, Secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s Political and Secret Department. At sixteen he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a cadet on the Grenville and heard Reginald Heber, the newly appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, preach. Afterwards he sought to win Muslims to a kinder view of Christians, which he saw as a first step towards spreading the Gospel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Conolly rose to captain in the 6th Bengal Light Cavalry and took part in many reconnaissance missions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, often in disguise under the name Khan Ali, a play on his own. In late 1829 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and Central Asia, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in September 1830 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in January 1831. His account of the journey, published in 1834, established his reputation as a traveller and writer.</p><p>In July 1840 he wrote to Major Henry Rawlinson, newly appointed political agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>: &quot;You've a great game, a noble game, before you.&quot; He hoped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> would cooperate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, make peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> to deal justly with his neighbours. In 1841 he tried without success to persuade the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to set aside their differences against Russian advance, and in November he was captured on his mission to free Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a>. Accused of spying, both men were beheaded in the square in front of the Ark of Bukhara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1845 Joseph Wolff, who had travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to learn the officers' fate and barely escaped with his life, published an account that made Conolly and Stoddart household names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for years. Conolly's elder brother, Henry Valentine Conolly, administrator of Malabar, was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1855. A portrait of Arthur Conolly by James Atkinson is in the National Portrait Gallery, and his diaries of 1840 to 1842, with letters and reports to Sir John Hobhouse and William Cabell, are in the British Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Conolly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emir Nasrullah executed the British officers Stoddart and <b>Conolly</b> in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bukhara">Emirate of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1829" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur Conolly"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1829">What was here in 1829? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
